    "llmlang.cli.main": {
      "file": "llmlang/cli.py",
      "text": "+ parses argv into a command and validated flags, then dispatches to check, build, or finalize\naccepts no arguments; reads argv directly\nparses argv into a command and validated flags, then dispatches to check, build, or finalize\n--help or -h anywhere in argv, or no arguments at all, prints usage\n--version prints the installed package version, falling back to a bundled version string when llmlang isn't actually installed\nrejects a command outside {check, build, finalize}, and rejects any flag that isn't valid for the given command (--coverage on build, say, or --config on finalize) - nothing is silently ignored\n--config and --output-format each consume the next argument as their value",
      "text_hash": "7a38d673d1cc1f50ada5c46f3a6a434c50f4f986ab2656a8d2d2cff2569b5fc9",
      "code_hash": "16aa05d4a37c0918ff196505518d96256b12ab046eef83ddd0883e112cefa25c",
      "last_change": {
        "disposition": "new entry: moved build.py's CLI logic into the llmlang package as cli.py, as part of packaging this as a real pip-installable tool with a console-script entry point. Previously build.py was explicitly excluded from self-hosting (treated as \"just the CLI wrapper\"); on reflection that exemption was weaker than the ones used elsewhere (e.g. for one-line private helpers) since this code encodes real, user-facing behavioral contracts - flag-vs-config precedence, what --output-format json's shape actually is, how multiple paths merge into one report. Rather than carry forward an informal, unconfigured exemption, every function here now has either a real handle+entry or an explicit [llm-exempt] marker (_plural, _usage) - the same rule everything else in this codebase already follows. No behavior changed from the pre-move build.py, only usage/help text (build.py -> llmlang) and the leading underscore dropped from functions that now have real entries, reserving it for the two that stayed exempt.",
